mplete (the fp
monitor's runs are observed at ~1–2 minutes). The
gap bounds below are therefore applied to
actual-to-actual gaps with a **+10-minute
tolerance** (≥ the documented early dispatch plus
observed run durations, with margin): a gap breaches
its bound only if it exceeds **2× the phase interval
+ 10 minutes**. Nominal-point matching uses the same
±10-minute tolerance. Jitter inside tolerance is not
a missed check and is never reported as one.

**Missed-check detection and the escalation
ladder.** A missed check is **detected at the next
performed check** — ordinarily the next scheduled
run — by the log gap against the nominal points,
resolved into its shape by the cross-check above.
On detection, the performing check (a) logs the gap
as an unobserved interval with its shape, (b)
performs the current check in full, and (c) applies
the ladder:

- **One missed/failed check, gap within bound**
  (bound = 2× the scheduled interval for that phase
  + the 10-minute tolerance: >2 h 10 min in the
  hourly phase, >12 h 10 min in the 6-hourly phase,
  >30 min in a tightened phase): logged and reported
  to Julie at the next report point; cadence
  continues; coverage qualification per above.
- **Escalation bound breached** — two consecutive
  missed/failed checks, **or** any single gap
  exceeding its bound, **or** reads unavailable
  across two consecutive performed checks: the
  performing check **escalates to Julie immediately**
  (delivery to the Coding Projects chat) **and the
  matter is filed for Rowan**, and Wren declares the
  window **HELD** (below) without waiting for
  instruction.

**The stop/hold decision rule — WINDOW-FAILED.**
Wren **must** declare the window WINDOW-FAILED when
the escalation bound is breached and coverage
cannot be re-established by the next scheduled
check, or when performed checks show the §3.1 set
persistently unreadable (two consecutive failed
checks on the same surface). The declaration is
Wren's duty under this rule, not a discretionary
judgment; it is written into the window log by the
declaring run (or in session, if detected there) and
delivered immediately; Julie may also declare it at
any time, and Rowan may rule it on the record.
Effects of WINDOW-FAILED:

- The window's observation posture is **hold**: no
  classification is upgraded on the strength of
  failed coverage; the event, if any, stands
  **undispositioned**.
- **C3 is blocked** (§4.3): WINDOW-FAILED is not a
  disposition.
- S1-X is **not** extended by the failure: its
  binding is time-based (§3.6) and expires at window
  close regardless. During unobserved intervals S1-X
  is in practice blind (it fires only on a verified
  observation by a performed check, §3.4) — named
  here so no one mistakes an armed S1-X for coverage.
- Both window jobs are **disabled at the
  declaration** (lifecycle, above); no tail job is
  created.
- Resumption (a renewed window, a re-based
  disposition, or any onward step) requires
  **Rowan's ruling + a fresh Julie authorization**.
  Nothing in Part B self-heals a failed window.

**Boundary — the vehicle never acts.** The watch
jobs **observe, log, classify, and escalate only**.
A watch job never performs a containment transition
(no `contain_drive`, no `stop_chain`), never sends
a command, and never fires S1-X. If a performed
check verifies a T1–T4 trigger, the check records
the firing decision in the window log (§3.6) and
escalates immediately to Julie in the Coding
Projects chat; **the S1-X act itself is performed by
Wren in session** on that recorded decision, under
§3.6's completion discipline. Layer-1 (B2), if
granted, is likewise performed in session on the
logged classification, never by a watch job. The
firing decision and any transition are
human-reviewable acts on a written record; the
vehicle's function is to make that record complete
and timely — not to act on it.

**Cost (visible, per RM-1).** Each scheduled run is
one agent task and consumes work allowance. Expected
counts for the 72-hour design: **1 smoke run**;
**≈22 scheduled window checks** (hourly phase ≈12,
including the handoff run; 6-hourly phase ≈10,
including the closing census). Supervised tightened
and stimulus-offset checks are in-session work, not
scheduler runs. Tail: Branch 1 daily (≤14 runs per
14-day tail period), Branch 2 weekly (≈2 per
period), Branch 3 daily until C3's preflight opens
or R3-V is authorized. A materially higher count
(e.g., repeated handoff failures) is reported, not
absorbed silently.

**Limitations (stated, not papered over).**

- **L-1 — Runs can fail or be delayed.** A runtime
  restart/replacement or a scheduler fault can kill
  or delay a scheduled run. The design **detects**
  this (log gaps + the run-history cross-check) but
  cannot **prevent** it; coverage claims span
  verified intervals only. Detection happens at the
  next performed check — late in a 6-hourly phase, a
  failure may go undetected for hours. That latency
  is inherent to the cadence and is named, not
  smoothed over.
- **L-2 — Dispatch jitter.** Cadence is nominal;
  coverage is actual (tolerance above). The design
  does not claim minute-exact scheduling.
- **L-3 — Escalation reaches Julie only when she
  views the chat.** Escalation delivery lands in the
  Coding Projects chat; there is **no paging
  guarantee**, and an overnight escalation may sit
  unread for hours. Consequence, stated plainly:
  **S1-X response latency after a recorded firing
  decision is not bounded by this design.** §3.6
  imposes a completion discipline (first in-session
  opportunity + a fresh-confirmation guard), and the
  exposure during the interval is bounded **in
  effect** (§7.3 churn under the holds — $0, no
  dispatch, delivery fenced) but **not in time**. If
  Rowan requires a hard completion bound, it must be
  imposed by ruling (§7, Item RM-1-c); this proposal
  does not invent one, and §3.6 cross-references
  this limitation where the B′ condition is applied.
- **L-4 — Allowance contention.** Each run consumes
  work allowance (counts above). A window coinciding
  with heavy other work could starve a run; a starved
  run presents as a failed/missed run and is handled
  by the ladder — detection, not prevention, again.
- **L-5 — Reads can fail.** The scheduler proves
  execution and logging of checks; it does not make
  production reads infallible. A run whose reads fail
  is a FAILED check under the coverage law; two
  consecutive failures on a surface feed the
  WINDOW-FAILED rule.
- **L-6 — Supervised portions depend on attended
  availability.** Tightened cadences, stimulus-offset
  reads, and Stage-4 record work are performed in
  session. Where a supervised check is not performed,
  the log shows the gap and coverage accounting
  governs; a supervised check is never back-filled
  from the base cadence after the fact.

**Reporting duty (unchanged in substance).** Wren
reports to Julie **immediately** on any
classification other than W-0, on any missed/failed
check that breaches a bound, on any W-1 → W-2/W-3/
W-4 transition, and on any declaration under this
section. W-0 checks are silent — logged, not
reported; under the vehicle, a W-0 run's chat
delivery states no escalation.

### 3.3 Classification taxonomy (RS-4: uncertainty classes resolved)

Every performed check is classified into exactly
one class. Paired surfaces must agree; a value that
cannot be freshly read is not evidence (§3.2's
failed-check rule governs check-level read
failures; this taxonomy classifies what completed
reads show).

- **W-0 — NO EVENT.** Every read at baseline.
  *Continue cadence.* (Span claims per §3.2
  coverage accounting.)
- **W-1 — WAKE OBSERVED, PASS PENDING.** `armedAt`
  changed to a new value V (V **is** D, made
  observable by the wake), no pass yet observed.
  **W-1p** — V past-due or near: tightened cadence.
  **W-1f** — V in the future (B3 re-base): recorded
  with V; not a completed event (§3.5).
